---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import NnMasthead from '../../components/NnMasthead.astro';
import race from '../../content/race.json';
import { orTbc } from '@src/shared/privacy';

// The same event theme as the forms it belongs to — see packages/shared/styles/nn-theme.css.
//
// **The horror stops at the edge of the card.** The page furniture is the campaign's, so
// somebody arriving from a form knows they have not left it; the notice itself is ink on
// white, Inter throughout, with nothing decorative anywhere near the legal text.
import '@src/shared/styles/nn-theme.css';

// The race's privacy notice.
//
// ## The club maintains this page now, and that changed on 30 August 2026
//
// **The committee supplied this notice as a document, and it was published word for word from
// 30 August 2026 until later the same day.** Issue #168 is where that ended: the collection
// list said the club holds a postal address and an expected finish time, which it has never
// asked anybody for, and did not say it holds medical information, a declaration that somebody
// is visually impaired, how a runner describes their gender, or anything at all about a guide —
// all four of which it does hold. Over-claiming is inaccurate; under-claiming what is special
// category data under Article 9 is the half that matters, and **the guide is the sharpest case
// of it**: a second person whose data is collected through somebody else's form, who may never
// read this page because they did not do the entering.
//
// **So the club took the edits itself rather than waiting for new wording, and that is a choice
// rather than a drift.** The alternative was the committee supplying an amended document, which
// is still the better route for anything that changes what the notice *says*; what was done
// here is narrower — **items inserted into and removed from one list, and no sentence anywhere
// on the page rewritten, restyled or reordered.** The document's own words, structure,
// capitalisation and headings are untouched. A change beyond that shape goes back to the
// committee.
//
// ⚠️ **On 31 August 2026 the shape was widened, deliberately and once — issue #179 items 4 and
// 5.** Until then the permitted edit was an item in *the collection list*, section 2. Two of
// the four things #179 left open are sentences the club has already committed to elsewhere and
// which section 2 alone cannot carry: the affiliation reservation decision 007 requires on both
// notices, and an Article 9 condition for the health data section 2 lists. **Both went in as
// list items, in their own list's existing shape** — one in section 2, one in section 4's list
// of legal bases — and still no sentence anywhere on this page has been rewritten, restyled or
// reordered. So the rule now reads: **an item inserted into or removed from a list, in that
// list's own voice, and nothing else.** Prose is still the committee's.
//
// **Neither item is new wording, which is what made it a transcription rather than a drafting
// exercise.** The affiliation item is the sentence already published on `/privacy/`, carried
// across word for word because decision 007 asks for it on both notices and it was on one. The
// Article 9 item names the condition the entry form already relies on — the medical note and
// the visually impaired declaration each take a separate, unbundled, explicit tick, and nothing
// is stored without one — so it describes the built consent rather than choosing a basis for it.
// **`nn-privacy.spec.ts`'s own heading test has claimed since 30 August that section 4 says
// "explicit consent is its basis" for the medical data. It did not, until this change.**
//
// **A third list edit was taken on 31 August 2026: photographs and video went in.** The entry
// terms have said since version 1 that a runner's name and photographic or video footage may be
// used to publicise the race and the club, and for the fancy dress competition — and this notice
// said nothing about images at all. Article 13 makes the categories of data mandatory
// information, so a processing activity the club states in the document somebody is bound by and
// omits from the notice describing that document is a gap rather than a nicety. The terms
// themselves are where the use is described; this list is where the club says it holds the data.
//
// ⚠️ **The opt-out those terms offer has no mechanism on this site.** "Tell us if you would
// rather not appear" is an instruction with no field, no consent box and no route except the
// contact address — a nineteenth entry field is a committee decision, so it is **not** built and
// this list item deliberately does not claim one exists.
//
// **A second list edit was taken on 31 August 2026, and it is a deletion — issue #179 item 1.**
// The collection list claimed *"IP address, browser type, device information, and cookies for
// website functionality and analytics"*. There is no analytics code anywhere in `apps/main` —
// no gtag, no Plausible, no Fathom — and a plain `GET /nn/2026/` sets no cookie at all, which
// is what `/privacy/` tells account holders in as many words. **So the club's two notices
// contradicted each other and the race one was the wrong half**, on the page that invites
// somebody to complain to the ICO about how their information is handled. The bullet is gone.
// It is a list item, so it is inside the same permitted shape as #168's insertions; nothing
// else on the page moved.
//
// **`/privacy/` is a separate document and was not touched by any of this.**
//
// ## `privacy.lastUpdated` is this page's revision date, not the committee document's
//
// The two were the same thing on 29 August 2026 and stopped being the same thing on the 30th,
// when the club took the first list edits and left the date alone. Section 9 of this notice
// promises that *"any changes will be posted on this page with an updated revision date"*, so
// a date that tracks the committee's draft rather than the published page is a promise the
// page breaks the moment a list item moves. **It tracks the page.** Every edit to what is
// rendered here — including a list item inserted or deleted — moves `race.json`'s
// `privacy.lastUpdated` in the same commit. Issue #179 item 3.
//
// ## What is still deliberately absent
//
// **An earlier version of this page merged the committee's document with the notice it
// replaced.** It carried a medical-note retention period, a schema-derived list of what the
// entry tables hold, the named processors, the registered office, and the affiliation sentence.
// All of it came out. Three of those differences are load-bearing elsewhere, so they are
// recorded here rather than left to be rediscovered:
//
//  1. **No retention period for a medical note is published *on this page*.**
//     `entries.events.medical_retention` is still one month and the cron still deletes on it,
//     and `packages/db/tests/entries-retention.test.ts` still ties that interval to
//     `race.json`'s `privacy.medicalRetention` — which is why that key is still there and
//     still reads "One month after the race" though this page no longer prints it. Naming the
//     medical box, as this page now does, does not restore it: what is collected and how long
//     it is kept are two claims, and only the first is back.
//
//     ⚠️ **What this file used to say next was that the period is published nowhere, and that
//     was never true** — issue #172. `/nn/2026/`, the page somebody ticks the medical consent
//     on, and `/account/data/` have both stated a period all along, in hand-typed prose that
//     nothing tied to the column. Since 31 August 2026 both derive it: the entry form from
//     `entry_state()`'s `medical_retention`, which is the enforced interval itself, and
//     `/account/data/` from `race.json`, which the database test above holds to that interval.
//     So the chain reaches a runner again — just not through this page.
//  2. **The named processors and the registered office are still absent.** The list below
//     names what is collected; who processes it is the committee's to supply.
//  3. **The affiliation sentence is here now, and it was the third of these until 31 August
//     2026.** ADR-023 and decision 007 ask for it on both notices and it was on one. It is a
//     collection-list item rather than a new sentence — the first half of it is a statement
//     about what the club does *not* collect, which is what section 2 is for, and the words are
//     `/privacy/`'s own. Issue #179 item 5, and issue #167's first half.
//
// ## What is interpolated, and why that is not an edit
//
// Four values come from `race.json` rather than being typed into the markup: the controller,
// the company number, the contact address and the date. **They render exactly the words the
// document uses.** `/privacy/` reads the same four, so retyping them here would let the club's
// two notices disagree about who the controller is — which has to be impossible rather than
// merely unlikely. `orTbc` renders "to be confirmed by the club" for a `null`, and none of
// these four is null, so this page carries no marker at all now.
//
// **A wrong answer on this page is a legal claim, not a typo.** See
// docs/architecture/principles.md#stop-and-ask

const canonical = 'https://new.southvillerunningclub.co.uk/nn/privacy/';

const { privacy } = race;
---

<Base
  title="What the club does with your details — Nightingale Nightmare"
  description="What Southville Running Club collects when you enter Nightingale Nightmare or register your interest, why, who else sees it, and how long it is kept."
  canonical={canonical}
  theme="nn"
>
  {
    /* The campaign navigation, as on the other four pages. Somebody who opens the privacy
    notice to check what is held before typing their name should not have to retrace their
    steps to carry on. */
  }
  <NnMasthead slot="masthead" />

  <header class="nn-hero nn-hero-compact">
    <div class="nn-wrap">
      <h1>What the club does with your details</h1>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="nn-wrap">
    <div class="nn-card nn-prose">
      {
        /* **`{' '}` is a real space, and it is here because a line break is not.** A newline
        either side of an expression is collapsed away rather than kept as a space, so a
        paragraph reads "…Nightingale Nightmareand nothing else" without it. Prettier is what
        moves a bare `{expression}` onto a line of its own, so a space typed in the source does
        not survive the next format — this page has now met that trap three times.
        `nn-privacy.spec.ts` asserts these joins as whole strings. */
      }
      <p class="lede">
        This covers the entry form and the register-your-interest form for{' '}
        {race.name}. Everything else the club does with your details — including an
        account on this website — is in <a href="/privacy/">the club's privacy notice</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>1. Who we are</h2>

      <p>
        {orTbc(privacy.controller)} is committed to protecting and respecting your privacy.
        For any personal data you provide for the purposes of registering your interest or entering{
          ' '
        }
        {race.name}, {orTbc(privacy.controller)}, Companies House company no.{' '}
        {orTbc(privacy.companyNumber)} (SRC) is the Data Controller and is responsible for storing
        and otherwise processing that data in a fair, lawful, secure and transparent way.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you are unhappy with how we have handled your information, please contact us at{
          ' '
        }
        {
          /* **A link only when there is an address to link to.** `orTbc` exists because these
          values can be `null`, and `href="mailto:null"` is a link that looks real, is reached
          by keyboard, and opens a message to nobody. The marker is not an address and must not
          be dressed as one. The link also carries `base.css`'s
          `a[href^='mailto:'] { overflow-wrap: anywhere }`, which is what stops a 31-character
          address scrolling the page sideways at 320px — the defect `/privacy/` met on the
          same value. */
        }
        {
          privacy.contact ? (
            <a href={`mailto:${privacy.contact}`}>{privacy.contact}</a>
          ) : (
            orTbc(privacy.contact)
          )
        }. You also have the right to complain to the Information Commissioner's Office{
          ' '
        }
        <a href="https://ico.org.uk/">https://ico.org.uk/</a>
      </p>

      <h2>2. Information we collect</h2>

      <p>
        We collect the following types of information to manage race registration and
        event logistics:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Personal Identification:</strong> Name, date of birth, gender, and how you
          describe your gender if you choose to tell us.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Contact Details:</strong> Email address, phone number.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Race-Related Information:</strong> Emergency contact details, running club
          affiliation, chosen race category.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Running Club Affiliation:</strong> If you take an affiliated entry, we do
          not ask for your England Athletics number. We do not collect one and we do not hold
          one — we take your word for it. We reserve the right to ask you to produce your registration
          number, or other evidence that you are affiliated, if we need to check that the entry
          fee you paid was the right one.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Health Information:</strong> Any medical information you choose to give us
          about yourself or about a guide running with you, under a separate consent; and, if
          you tell us you are visually impaired so that a guide can run with you, that declaration.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your Guide's Details:</strong> If a guide is running with you, their name,
          date of birth, email address and emergency contact details, collected on your entry.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Payment Information:</strong> Processed securely by our third-party payment
          provider; we do not store full card details.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Photographs and Video:</strong> Images and footage of participants taken at
          the event, which the entry terms permit us to use to publicise the race and the club
          and for the fancy dress competition on the day.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>3. How We Use Your Information</h2>

      <p>We use your information to:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Process your race registration and payment</li>
        <li>Communicate event updates and essential safety information</li>
        <li>Manage timing, results, and race-day logistics</li>
        <li>Provide customer support</li>
        <li>Improve website performance and user experience</li>
        <li>Comply with legal or regulatory obligations</li>
      </ul>

      <p>We do not sell your data to third parties.</p>

      <h2>4. Legal Basis For Processing</h2>

      <p>We process your data under the following legal bases:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Contractual necessity: To register you for the race and deliver event services
        </li>
        <li>
          Legitimate interests: Event safety, operational efficiency, and fraud prevention
        </li>
        <li>Consent: For optional marketing communications</li>
        <li>
          Explicit consent (Article 9(2)(a)): For the health information in section 2 —
          any medical information you give us, and telling us you are visually impaired.
          Each is asked for separately on the entry form, we hold neither unless you agree
          to it, and you can withdraw at any time
        </li>
        <li>Legal obligations: Health and safety requirements, incident reporting</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>5. Data Sharing</h2>

      <p>We may share your information with:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Timing and results providers</li>
        <li>Payment processors</li>
        <li>Medical or emergency services (only when necessary for safety)</li>
        <li>
          Our race is run under an ARC permit, so we may have to share limited details
          with the permitting body if there is an incident.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>6. Data Retention</h2>

      <p>
        We retain information only for as long as reasonably necessary for the purposes
        described in this policy, including legal, accounting, insurance,
        dispute-resolution, safety, and historical-results requirements.
      </p>

      <p>
        When the retention policy ends, we will securely delete the information, or remove
        any personally identifiable information.
      </p>

      <h2>7. Your rights</h2>

      <p>You can have the right to</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Access your personal data</li>
        <li>Request correction or deletion</li>
        <li>Object to processing</li>
        <li>Withdraw consent</li>
        <li>Request data portability</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        <strong
          >If you request data deletion, your information will be removed and your race
          entry will be cancelled.</strong
        > Requesting deletion of your data will not impact any future relationship with the
        SRC. Please contact {
          privacy.contact ? (
            <a href={`mailto:${privacy.contact}`}>{privacy.contact}</a>
          ) : (
            orTbc(privacy.contact)
          )
        }.
      </p>

      <h2>8. Photographs</h2>

      <p>
        Photographs will be taken throughout the event for the purposes of documenting
        race activities, celebrating participant achievements, and promoting future
        events. This may include images of runners, volunteers, spectators, and the
        general event environment. By attending you acknowledge that you may appear in
        these images. We may use selected photos on our website, social media channels,
        marketing materials, and partner platforms, but we will never use imagery in a
        misleading or harmful way. If you prefer not to be photographed, please inform us
        before the event and avoid posing for official photographers; however, incidental
        inclusion in wide-angle or crowd shots may still occur.
      </p>

      <h2>9. Changes to this policy</h2>

      <p>
        We may update this Privacy Policy periodically. Any changes will be posted on this
        page with an updated revision date.
      </p>

      <p>Last updated: {orTbc(privacy.lastUpdated)}</p>
    </div>

    {
      /* **The sign-off paragraph was removed on 30 August 2026, at the club's request.** It
      read "Anything above reading 'to be confirmed by the club' is with the committee, and this
      page is updated when it is settled" — a sentence describing a page with unanswered values
      on it. There are none left here, so it described nothing. The two links stay: a notice
      somebody cannot get back out of is a dead end. */
    }
    <footer>
      <p>
        <a href="/nn/">Back to {race.name}</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/privacy/">The club's privacy notice</a>
      </p>
    </footer>
  </div>

  {
    /* The same decorative artwork as the forms' page, and empty for the same reason — it is
    a CSS background. See the artwork section of nn-theme.css. */
  }
  <div class="nn-hands" aria-hidden="true"></div>
</Base>
